ncouraged to compare simulation results from AI/ML generalist models against eType-II and demonstrate a consistent performance delta over eType-II codebook feedback in order to conclude the feasibility of AI/ML test decoder derivation. Generalist model trained with mixed SLS and TDL data The cross-domain evaluation in Section 3.5 demonstrated that an SLS-only trained model exhibits significant SGCS degradation on TDL link-level data, motivating the inclusion of LLS data in the training dataset. In this section, we describe the training setup for a zero-pad generalist model trained on a mixed dataset combining both SLS (CDL) and LLS (TDL) eigenvector data across all port, sub-band, channel model, and SNR configurations. Mixed SLS + TDL dataset construction The mixed training dataset is constructed from 15 source files: 6 CDL (system-level) eigenvector datasets and 9 TDL (link-level) eigenvector datasets. The CDL data covers all 6 (N_TX, N_SB) combinations, while the TDL data covers 3 channel models (TDL-A, TDL-B, TDL-C) × 3 antenna configurations across 16-port and 32-port, all with 13 sub-bands and 3 SNR levels (3 dB, 10 dB, 20 dB). Table 5: Mixed SLS + TDL dataset sample counts by source type and split. Table 6 details the 9 TDL datasets included in the mixed training set. Each TDL file contains 90,000 raw channel realizations (30,000 per SNR level) with 4 eigenvectors each, yielding 360,000 expanded samples per file. The three SNR levels correspond to low (3 dB), medium (10 dB), and high (20 dB) operating points. Table 6: TDL link-level datasets in the mixed training set. Antenna config notation: (N_vertical, N_horizontal, N_pol). Each file contains 3 SNR levels × 30,000 raw samples × 4 EVs = 360,000 expanded samples. Table 7 provides the sub-band distribution in the training set. Since all 9 TDL files have 13 sub-bands, the 13 SB configuration is heavily represented (74.6% of training samples), while 6 SB and 18 SB configurations are exclusively from CDL data. Table 7: Sub-band distribution in the mixed training dataset. Data loading and preprocessing The CDL and TDL datasets follow different loading pipelines: • CDL loading: each .npy file is loaded and 4 eigenvectors per sample are expanded as independent training samples. A 50% subsample is applied, followed by capping at ~333k samples per configuration. The data is split sequentially into 80/10/10 train/validation/test sets. • TDL loading: each .npy file contains 90,000 raw samples (30,000 per SNR level) with 4 eigenvectors each, yielding 360,000 expanded samples per file. No subsampling is applied. To ensure balanced SNR representation, each SNR group (120k expanded samples) is split independently into 80/10/10 before concatenation, yielding 288k train / 36k val / 36k test per TDL file with exactly equal SNR representation in each split. All samples are zero-padded to the maximum configuration size [2, 18, 32] (real/imag, 18 sub-bands, 32 ports) and concatenated into a single mixed dataset. Per-sample metadata (N_TX, N_SB, TDL file ID, SNR index) is stored for loss masking during training and per-configuration filtering during evaluation. Architecture and training The model uses the same zero-pad architecture as described in Section 3.3, with the fixed-size transformer sized for maximum configuration (N_TX=32, N_SB=18, ld=192). The training procedure is also identical: each batch of 2048 samples (randomly mixed from all 15 source files) is forward-passed 5 times (once per latent dimension {32, 64, 96, 128, 192}), with masked SGCS loss computed only on the real (N_TX, N_SB) dimensions of each sample. Other hyperparameters remain unchanged: learning rate 1e-4 with cosine annealing, 150 epochs, 8-bit weight

