Dalian, CN, May 18–22, 2026 Agenda item: 6.1.2 Document for: Discussion Introduction In this contribution, we present system-level simulation results for AI/ML-based beam management under Case 3 (measurement errors applied to training data, inference input, and ground truth) for Scenario 2 (wide-to-narrow: 8 SSB TX beams → 32 CSI-RS TX beams). The simulation methodology follows the agreed assumptions in [1] and [2]. We evaluate beam prediction accuracy (KPI 1) and absolute RSRP accuracy (KPI 2) using a UMi deployment at 30 GHz, and report results against both ideal (genie clean) and noisy (genie noisy) ground truth. We also outline ongoing work on inference under multiple UE locations and on the evaluation of full CDL-C (24 clusters) versus simplified 6-cluster CDL models following the agreed 3-step evaluation framework, and propose acceptability criteria for simplified channel models used in conformance testing. Discussion Prior Agreements and Status At RAN4#118, the following key agreements relevant to beam management performance evaluation were reached [2]: • Channel model evaluation should be concluded at RAN4#119, based on available simulation results. Only previously agreed channel model simplifications and the agreed evaluation framework are considered. • The channel model simplification baseline is Option 2 (weak cluster simplification) with 6-cluster UMi CDL-C at 28 GHz. Table 9 (AoA aligned to probe layout, no intra-cluster angle spread) is the prioritized option; Table 8 (flat ZoA) is optional. The CASA value in Table 9 remains FFS. • The channel quality evaluation framework follows a 3-step procedure: (1) inference with reference CDL, (2) inference with simplified CDL, (3) compare. The same trained model is used for both steps, and results are averaged over multiple UE orientations. • Test setup: Set A = 32 beams, Set B = 8 beams. Statistics are based on data collected over all test iterations (not averaged separately per iteration). • UE rotation between test iterations is enabled; position is fixed during each test iteration. UE baseband SNR > −3 dB is required as a side condition for inference results. • Companies are invited to study whether UE location affects performance [2]. The present contribution addresses these agreements by providing Case 3 simulation results for Scenario 2 using the agreed UMi deployment assumptions, and outlines ongoing work on channel model simplification and UE location impact. Simulation Methodology Deployment and channel model System-level simulations follow the agreed assumptions in [1]. The deployment scenario is UMi (Urban Micro-cell) per TR 38.901, with a hexagonal layout of 7 sites and 3 sectors per site (21 cells). The key simulation parameters are summarized in Table 1. Table 1: System-level simulation parameters Beam configuration (Scenario 2) Scenario 2 uses wide-to-narrow beam prediction: Set B consists of 8 SSB TX beams (wide) and Set A consists of 32 CSI-RS TX beams (narrow). The beam configurations follow Table 7 of [1]. Table 2: Beam configuration for Scenario 2 (wide-to-narrow) The UE antenna panel structure is (M=1, N=4, P=2) with 2 panels (Mg=1, Ng=2), as specified in [1]. After polarization combining, the maximum RSRP across RX beams is selected per TX beam for both training and inference, yielding input dimension 8 and output dimension 32. Error model (Case 3) Case 3 applies measurement errors to training data, inference input, and ground truth. The error model combines RF errors and baseband errors as specified in [1]. Table 3: Error model for Case 3 RF and baseband errors are applied independently per TX beam, per RX beam, and per sample (i.i.d.). The baseband error distribution is obtained from link-level simulation with TDL-C channel at the a

