Malta, MT, April 13th – 17th, 2026 Agenda item: 7.12.3 Document for: Discussion Introduction In this contribution, we present simulation results for AI/ML-based RRM measurement prediction (RSRP) using a system-level simulator compliant with the 3GPP 38.901 channel model. We evaluate a lightweight LSTM predictor against the Sample-and-Hold (SnH) baseline across two UE speeds (60 and 90 km/h), analyse the physical channel characteristics that determine RSRP predictability, and compare our findings with results reported by other companies during RAN2 #127-bis evaluations. The key topics addressed in this contribution are: • Simulation setup aligned with the agreed WF from RAN4#118 [1] • LSTM prediction results at 30 km/h (K=5 horizons, 200 ms prediction window) and 90 km/h (K=10 horizons, 400 ms prediction window) • Channel predictability analysis: fading physics, LOS–NLOS power ratio (ΔP), and the predictability spectrum of RSRP components • MMSE prediction framework: three-bound error decomposition (S&H upper bound, MMSE lower bound, exploitable gap) and the AI/ML efficiency metric η(k) • Impact of spatial consistency procedure (Procedure A vs B) on prediction limits • Cross-company comparison with RAN2 #127-bis evaluation results • Impact of measurement errors (baseband + RF noise) on prediction performance at 30 km/h and 90 km/h, including comparison of noisy-output vs clean-output training • Proposals for alignment of simulation methodologies and investigation of fundamental prediction limits Discussion In the WF from RAN4#118 [1], it was agreed to study simulation assumptions for AI/ML-based mobility enhancements, including RRM measurement prediction. The simulation parameters, evaluation metrics, and baseline definitions follow the framework established in TR 38.843 [2] and the agreed simulation assumptions WF [3]. Simulation Setup We use a system-level simulator (CSLS) implementing the 3GPP TR 38.901 channel model with spatial consistency (Procedure A). The simulation parameters are summarized in Table 1. Table 1: Simulation parameters AI/ML Model and Baseline We adopt a single-layer LSTM as the prediction model with the following configuration: Table 2: LSTM model configuration The model takes L=5 consecutive RSRP samples (in dBm) as input and outputs K predicted future RSRP values. Input standardization (zero-mean, unit-variance) is applied using training set statistics. The baseline is Sample-and-Hold (SnH): the last observed RSRP value is repeated for all K prediction horizons. This represents the simplest non-trivial baseline and is the agreed reference in the RAN4 WF [3]. Dataset Construction RSRP measurements are generated from 20 independent simulation seeds. Each seed produces 250 time steps (10 s at 40 ms intervals) across 21 cells and 30 UEs, yielding 12,600 link trajectories per speed. Sliding windows of length L+K are extracted from each trajectory, with the first L samples as input and the remaining K as prediction targets. The total number of sliding windows exceeds 3 million per speed configuration, split 70/15/15 into training, validation, and test sets with no seed overlap. Prediction Results at 30 km/h Table 3 shows the per-horizon error statistics for the LSTM predictor and the SnH baseline at 30 km/h with K=5 prediction horizons (200 ms prediction window). The evaluation metric is the absolute prediction error |y_true − y_pred| in dB. Table 3: Per-horizon prediction error statistics at 30 km/h (K=5) The LSTM achieves 48–68% median error reduction over SnH across all prediction horizons. At k=1 (40 ms), the median error is only 0.036 dB, confirming that short-horizon prediction in FR1 at moderate speed is essentially solved. Even at k=5 (200 ms), the LSTM median erro

